@startuml runtime-04-first-run
' Runtime scenario 4 - first run and profile resolution.
' Canonical source for docs/architecture/arc42.md section 6.4.
' This is the path every command shares before it does anything specific, and the
' one that decides which chain list the rest of the invocation fans out across.

title Runtime scenario 4 - first run, and which profile a command uses

actor Operator
participant "index.ts" as Boot
participant "lib/env" as Env
participant "lib/chains" as Ch
participant "lib/profiles" as Pf
database "config directory" as Dir
database "bundled profile" as Bundle

== Module evaluation, before any command runs ==

Operator -> Boot: evm - any subcommand at all
activate Boot
Boot -> Env: import
activate Env
Env -> Env: findPackageRoot() - walk up from this file until\nconfig/default-profile.yaml is found
note right of Env
  A fixed relative path would not work: this
  module sits at src/lib when run from source
  through tsx, and at dist/src/lib once compiled.
  Failing to find it is the documented
  "Could not locate bundled config/default-profile.yaml".
end note
Env -> Env: USER_CONFIG_DIR = EVM_ELF_CONFIG_DIR,\nelse XDG_CONFIG_HOME/evm-elf,\nelse ~/.config/evm-elf
note right of Env
  Computed at module evaluation, which is why
  neither of those two variables can come from
  a .env file: the path of the .env is derived
  from them. The dependency is circular and is
  resolved in the documentation, not in code.
end note
Env --> Boot: path constants
deactivate Env

== Environment, loaded exactly once ==

Boot -> Env: loadEnv()
activate Env
Env -> Dir: dotenv ./.env, then the config directory .env
note right of Env
  dotenv never overwrites a variable that is
  already set, so precedence is:
  real environment, then project .env,
  then user .env.
end note
Dir --> Env: variables merged into process.env
Env --> Boot: done
deactivate Env

== Which profile, and why that one ==

Boot -> Ch: handler calls loadProfile(options.profile)
activate Ch
alt -p given
  Ch -> Ch: use that name or path
else nothing given
  Ch -> Env: resolveDefaultProfile()
  activate Env
  Env -> Env: EVM_ELF_PROFILE wins
  Env -> Dir: else read profiles/.default
  Dir --> Env: pointer contents, or nothing
  Env -> Env: else the built-in name "default"
  note right of Env
    Returns the source alongside the name.
    Carrying the provenance is what lets
    "Profile not found" say where the name
    came from, and lets evm profile list
    explain its own answer.
  end note
  Env --> Ch: name and source
  deactivate Env
end

Ch -> Ch: resolveProfilePath() - a bare name is checked against the\npermitted pattern and looked up in the profiles directory,\nwith a .yml fallback; anything path-like is used as given

alt file missing and the name is exactly "default"
  Ch -> Pf: ensureDefaultProfile(path)
  activate Pf
  Pf -> Bundle: copyFile with COPYFILE_EXCL
  note right of Pf
    Never overwrites, so a concurrent run
    that got there first wins. copyFile
    carries the source permissions across
    and the bundled profile is world-readable,
    so every copy is chmod 0600 afterwards.
  end note
  Bundle --> Pf: 14 chains, one explorers entry
  Pf -> Dir: write profiles/default.yaml at mode 0600
  Pf -> Operator: "Created ... from the bundled default profile" on stderr
  Pf --> Ch: seeded
  deactivate Pf
else file missing and the name is anything else
  Ch -> Operator: "Profile not found: PATH", naming where the name came from
end

Ch -> Dir: read and validate the profile
Dir --> Ch: chains mapping, optional explorers mapping
note right of Ch
  An unknown key under a chain rejects the
  whole file. An unknown key at the top level
  is ignored, which is why a misspelled
  "chains" reads as a missing one.
end note
Ch --> Boot: RpcProfile - the chain list the rest of the run fans out across
deactivate Ch
deactivate Boot

@enduml
